This module is designed to introduce basic yet fundamental elements of Maltese grammar in a gradual and comprehensive manner. Participating students will be introduced to particular and unique features of the Maltese language which are omnipresent in texts as well as in speech.

Unit 1 – The Definite Article in Maltese and its Usage 
Unit 2 – Combination of Definite with Maltese Prepositions
Unit 3 – Maltese Prepositions + Pronominal Suffixes
Unit 4 – Marking Possession in Maltese
Unit 5 – Expressing the Verb to be in Maltese & Other Basic Verbal Notions
Unit 6 – The Gender of Maltese Nouns
Unit 7 – Demonstrative Words
a)	a better understanding of the way how Maltese is written;
b)	basic grammatical competence with many crucial yet special aspects of the Maltese language;
c)	a deeper knowledge of the Maltese sound and orthographic features such as consonant assimilation, euphonic changes, as well as punctuation marks like the sing and the apostrofu (both for contractions and representation of the għ. a)	use the definite article in Maltese;
b)	learn Maltese prepositions and their usages;
c)	formulate short equational sentences;
d)	differences between Maltese pronouns and those in other languages;
e)	become more self-reliant in formulating sentences in Maltese. a)	distinguishing solar versus lunar consonants;
b)	combined prepositions versus non-combined;
c)	pronominal suffixes that mark possession or relationship;
d)	copular words for the present to be and basic usages of kien;
e)	suffixes and the gender assigned to them;
f)	proximal and distal demonstrative words;
g)	noun-type and adjective-type sentences using vocabulary introduced in Modules 1 & 2.

This course does not lead to a particular occupation. However, it provides the necessary nutritional background to support decision-making in enhancing fitness. In particular, targeted individuals are athletes, sports coaches, fitness instructors, and others who want to make a conscious effort to improve their health and well-being. 

It may also add to the knowledge background of those reading qualifications in Health and Social Care, and Hospitality, and who have a desire to help their clients improve their dietary habits.
The learner will be able to: 

a) name the main macro and micro nutrients needed for healthy bodily functions;
b) identify the nutrients and associated food sources required to enhance the differing components of fitness;
c) define digestion, absorption and assimilation;
d) label the human digestive system;
e) match the parts of the digestive system to their respective function;
f) name legal supplements and ergogenic aids commonly used by athletes in enhancing fitness and performance;
g) name national and international agencies responsible for anti-doping;
h) list the components of food labels;
i) write a food diary;
j) understand the role of the nutritionist. At the end of this programme, the learner will be able to:

a) design meals to enhance different components of fitness;
b) practice competition nutrition strategies;
c) use knowledge of the human digestive system in decisions regarding nutrition;
d) plan appropriate volumes of water intake to fit a particular training session/competitive event;
e) demonstrate knowledge of artificial supplements and other ergogenic aids;
f) show awareness of the agencies and associated policies responsible for anti-doping procedures;
g) use the information on food labels to assemble meals accoding to certain calorific and nutritional requirements;
h) show awareness of the concept of motivation and its role in maintaining high levels of sports fitness.

This unit will allow students to realise the importance of possessing digital literacy skills and that their digital presence impacts their overall credibility and employability chances. It will also allow students to understand how they can use their digital platforms to get their voices heard and get an informal basic introduction to marketing.

More specifically, the digital component of this module will allow students to gain an insight into different computer systems and networks, computer software, social media platforms, privacy and security methods, how they are used and what their purpose is in both personal and professional life. 

In addition to digital abilities, individuals also need mathematical confidence and good numeracy skills in order to overcome certain barriers in the place of work. Numeracy skills are not just needed for our work life, but also in our personal life. This module will therefore allow individuals to enhance their numeracy skills, find the use of numeracy skills in their everyday life and help them gain mathematical confidence.
At the end of the module/unit the learner will have acquired the following skills: 

a. Demonstrate understanding of computers, computer networks and computer software and how they are used;
b. Show awareness of the effective use of the internet to source and store information;
c. Use emails and social media platforms to communicate and create content;
d. Exhibit understanding of the importance of social media presence in today’s professional world;
e. Outline different ways to be able to successfully protect oneself online;
f. Demonstrate an understanding of how information technology and digital platforms are used to store, retrieve and send information;
g. Understand and appreciate the role of numeracy skills in personal and professional life;
h. Use the taught numeracy skills for problem solving;
i. Understand how numeracy skills can be used to communicate in a creative, logical and precise manner;
j. Apply calculations to obtain results related to money, time, temperatures etc;
k. Demonstrate ability to use different units of measurement to measure and present objects, diagrams and plans; At the end of the module/unit the learner will have acquired the responsibility and autonomy to:
a)	Carry out the installation as well as the configuration of different basic computer systems;
b)	Ensure appropriate choice of computer peripheral systems depending on the requirements and scenario;
c)	Set-up different user-groups, sharing permissions and third-party measures;
d)	Carry out tasks using different computer software to design and present one’s work like text document, presentations and spreadsheets;
e)	Create and modify information through presentations, spreadsheets, and word documents using the right digital tools;
f)	Prepare personal documents such as CV and covering letters making use of digital applications;
g)	Monitor one self’s digital presence and social media presence;
h)	Use information technology in a secure manner to ensure privacy and safety of oneself and others;
i)	Carry out a range of calculations using decimals, fractions and whole members;
j)	Manage calculations using the different arithmetical operations (addition, subtraction, multiplication and division);
k)	Carry out tasks and problem solving techniques using basic mathematical skills in real life; At the end of the module/unit the learner will have been exposed to the following: 

a)	Demonstrate understanding of the computer and computer systems
-	What is a Computer;
-	Computer Software and Hardware;
-	Information Networks;
-	Safety and Security in Computers and Networks;
b)	Practice storing Data in a Computer;
-	Basic Functions and the Operating System;
-	Managing Files in a Computer;
-	Manage Data and Information on the Computer;
c)	Practice Computer Networks
-	The basic concepts of computer networking (LAN/WAN) and set-up (Wireless/Wired/Range/Repeaters/Access Points/Bandwith)
-	Computer Networks Security (firewalls/malware protection plan)
d)	Create Digital Content
-	Word Processing;
-	Databases;
-	Spreadsheets;
-	Presentations
d)	Practice Communicating Using Digital Channels;
-	Introducing the Internet;
e)	Demonstrate the following numerical knowledge:
-	Define Integers and Numerical Sequences;
-	Describe Fractions and Decimal Numbers;
-	Identify various Numerical Operations and Indices;
-	Define and Use Percentages;
-	List Ratios and Proportions;
-	Measures, Scales and Money;
-	Sequence Estimation and Approximations;
-	Describe The Use of the Calculator;
-	Identify Algebraic Representations;
-	Sequence Equations and Inequalities;
-	Sequence Formulae and Graphs;
-	Draw Angles, Lines and Shapes;
-	Identify Symmetry and Congruency;
-	Identify Trigonometry, Bearings and Transformation Geometry
-	Write Statistics and Probability;

This course is aimed at providing the learner with knowledge, skills and competences required to work as Refrigeration and Air-conditioning Maintenance Personnel. Through this training programme, the learner will be able to identify and understand the correct practices used in this industry. It is also aimed at raising a sense of Health & Safety, Environmental Awareness, Customer Service and Work Communication.
The learner will be able to: 

a)	Use the relevant technologies to handle fluorinated greenhouse gases safely; 
b)	Apply work practices that comply with legislation on RAC systems.
c)	Identify, discuss and report a fault within a RAC system;
d)	Advise on the potential leakage points of refrigeration, air-conditioning and heat pump equipment;
e)	Carry out visual and manual inspections of the whole system for standard leakage requirements on RAC systems containing fluorinated greenhouse gases, so as to comply with local and EU regulations;
f)	Use industry standard recording of data in equipment records;
g)	Weigh refrigerant to the specified decimal place using the correct type of scale;
h)	Dismantle and reassemble components to drain/flush oil out of system;
i)	Calculate the quantity of flushing agent required;
j)	Determine the type of contaminant within the system;
k)	Select the appropriate procedure and agent to flush the system;
l)	Decide when to declare a system clean of contaminants. The learner will be able to: 

a)	Understand the importance of maintaining and using tools and equipment in a manner which complies with health and safety requirements;
b)	Be familiar with handling procedures relating to hazardous materials; 
c)	Know about the use and safe handling of fluorinated greenhouse gases and other substances as refrigerants;
d)	Identify possible Fluorinated-gas replacement gases;
e)	Be familiar with legislation relevant to RAC systems, their installation, repair, maintenance and decommission;
a)	List the basic ISO standard units for temperature, pressure, mass density & energy;
b)	Name and describe the function of different components of a RAC system including but not limited to; Compressors, condensers and evaporators;
c)	List the different types of AC filters;
d)	List the copper pipe categories of RAC;
e)	Recall procedures to connect and disconnect the equipment with minimal emissions;
f)	Recall the important precautions required when taking readings from a scale in order to obtain accurate results; 
g)	List the type of Ozone friendly flushing agent.
